give us your thoughts on how much the  questions that are being raised by VW in  the United States and the tip of the ice  but what does this mean for Europe I  think they're the tip of the iceberg  here in Europe we've seen a similar very  big gap between the official test  results which happen in a laboratory and  the way that cars actually perform on  the road in terms of their nitrogen  oxide emissions a study we launched last  week showed that just one in ten new  European diesel cars was meeting the new  regulatory limits that every vehicle  should have met on the first of sep  tember this year and some vehicles were  producing substantially more pollution  than what they were permitted to we're  talking about 10 or 20 times and the  questions therefore how are these  vehicles passing their laboratory tests  when they're performing so poorly on the  road so I would be very surprised if we  didn't see over the the forthcoming days  and months more manufacturers identified  as fitting these types of defeat devices  to trick the tests if that turns out to  be the case and we're dealing with  millions of cars what should the correct  response be to that the pollution that  is produced by these diesel engines is a  is a major issue for European  governments right now what response  should those governments generate as a  result of this well the pollution  generated is is leading to the premature  deaths of over half a million Europeans  every year it's costing the European  economy nearly a trillion euros and  certainly in the UK they recently said  that eighty percent of the problem in  cities was coming from diesels so the  diesel engine is a huge contributor to a  massive public health crisis in Europe  and I think the response from  governments is is very clear and indeed  from the European Commission we need to  have a really thorough investigation to  identify all of the ways in which these  defeat devices are being used  all of the manufacturers which are using  them they may not be limited just a  diesel cars but secondly we really need  the existing testing system tightened up  enormously in Europe we don't test the  same cars that people actually buy the  tests are completely unrepresentative of  the way the cars are driven on the road  and the people that do the testing are  paid for by the manufacturers and so are  those that oversee the tests we need an  independent regulator in Europe like we  do in the US this is the why this is the  reason the US authorities identify this  problem whereas in Europe we sell 50  times more vehicles but it wasn't found  here so Greg who are you calling on to  take action in this in Europe then I  think it has to be a lead from the  European Commission they have the  responsibility to bring forward new  regulations as part of the system of  type approval through which all vehicles  are are checked as fit for purpose  they're the people that must first take  action but I think there's an important  responsibility on the car industry also  VW has actually been very open since the  initial disclosure that they were  fitting these devices i'd like to see  other parts of the car industry be as  opens VW acknowledge the fact that they  are also using these technologies and  then we can begin resolve the problem  and move on I have to cut you off Greg  Archer thank you very much indeed for  your time
 

